---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../components/layout/BaseLayout.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/coderturtle/copilot-fluent";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const recentEntries = (await getCollection("buildlog", ({ data }) => !data.draft))
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf())
  .slice(0, 3);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Copilot Fluent: guide"
  description="A self-paced workshop that gates you on producing real work with Microsoft 365 Copilot, not reading about it."
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Copilot Fluent</h1>
  <p class="mt-2 text-lg opacity-80">
    Get fluent with the Copilot your company already pays for.
  </p>

  <div class="post-body mt-10">
    <p>
      Most enterprise Microsoft 365 Copilot seats get used as a fancier search box. This workshop
      gates you on producing real work with it instead, across nine modules, inside one continuing
      fictional workplace. No git, no command line, no coding background assumed. Read a module
      here, then do the exercise in your own M365 apps.
    </p>

    <h2>Who this is for</h2>
    <p>
      Non-technical knowledge workers with a licensed enterprise Microsoft 365 Copilot seat (the
      paid add-on, not the free baseline Copilot Chat). Comfort with Word, Excel, PowerPoint,
      Outlook, and Teams at a normal daily-user level is all that's assumed.
    </p>

    <h2>Two checks, every module</h2>
    <p>
      First, a <strong>required-output checklist</strong>: did you actually produce the thing,
      with the properties it needed - a specific figure, a word count, a matching data point.
      Checkable by counting, searching, or comparing, not a subjective "is this good" call.
      Second, an <strong>AI-graded rubric</strong>: you paste your work against a supplied grading
      prompt inside Copilot itself and get feedback. This second check is a new idea for this
      workshop and untested against a real learner yet, named honestly rather than oversold.
    </p>

    <h2>Why AB-730</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/ai-business-professional/">
        AB-730, "Microsoft Certified: AI Business Professional,"
      </a>{" "}
      is a real, current, no-code Microsoft certification. This workshop's module arc is designed
      to map onto its published skills outline rather than inventing its own sequence. It's
      designed to prepare you to sit that exam, not a guarantee - completing this workshop on its
      own doesn't credential anything; AB-730 is a separate, real, proctored exam you still have to
      sit.
    </p>

    <h2>The runbook</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Prerequisites: a licensed enterprise Microsoft 365 Copilot seat, and daily-user comfort with Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, and Teams.</li>
      <li>
        Start at <a href={`${base}modules/`}>the modules index</a>. It has the full arc, in order,
        with what each one is about - the module pages themselves are still being built (see
        Current status, below).
      </li>
      <li>
        Work through the nine modules in order. Module 03 is a hard prerequisite for Modules 04
        through 08 - every later exercise assumes the prompt-construction skill it teaches.
      </li>
      <li>
        No module completes by reading it. Every module states a required gate, checked two ways.
        If a module ever reduces to "read this, then move on," or a link here is broken, that's a
        defect: <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>open an issue</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Current status</h2>
    <p>
      All nine modules are skeleton only: a decided question, arc position, gate design, and
      takeaway shape, but no authored exercise yet. That's real work still ahead, not a formality.
      Watch the build log below for progress.
    </p>
  </div>

  {
    recentEntries.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h2 class="mt-14 text-xl font-semibold">Recent from the build log</h2>
        <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
          {recentEntries.map((entry) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`${base}build-log/${entry.id}/`} class="entry-card">
                <p class="entry-title text-lg font-semibold">{entry.data.title}</p>
                <p class="mt-1 text-sm opacity-80">{entry.data.description}</p>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-6 text-sm opacity-70">
          See the <a href={`${base}build-log/`} class="underline">full index</a>.
        </p>
      </>
    )
  }
</BaseLayout>
